h species and location; the greatest diversity of these is in North America."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-clams"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clams"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"]]],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"]]],["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Amêijoas à Bulhão Pato","dish/ameijoas-a-bulhao-pato"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Clam","ingredient/clam"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["White wine","ingredient/white-wine"],["Dungeness crab","ingredient/dungeness-crab"],["Mussel","ingredient/mussel"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Scallops","ingredient/scallops"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Squid as food","ingredient/squid-as-food"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Tomatoes","ingredient/tomatoes"],["Wine","ingredient/wine"]]}]},"ingredient/blood-as-food":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/blood-as-food","n":"Blood as food","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 8 cuisines","intro":[["Blood is used as food in many religions and cultures, often in combination with meat. The blood may be in the form of blood sausage or other solidified form, as a thickener for sauces, a cured salted form for times of food scarcity, or in a blood soup. This is a product from domesticated animals, obtained at a place and time where the blood can run into a container and be swiftly consumed or processed. In many cultures, the animal is slaughtered. In some cultures and religions, blood is a taboo food."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-blood-as-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blood_as_food"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":8,"groups":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Black pudding","dish/black-pudding"]]],["Finnish cuisine","cuisine/finnish-cuisine",[["Blodpalt","dish/blodpalt"]]],["Irish cuisine","cuisine/irish-cuisine",[["Black pudding","dish/black-pudding"]]],["Lao cuisine","cuisine/lao-cuisine",[["Nam tok (food)","dish/nam-tok-food"]]],["Latvian cuisine","cuisine/latvian-cuisine",[["Blodpalt","dish/blodpalt"]]],["Scottish cuisine","cuisine/scottish-cuisine",[["Stornoway black pudding","dish/stornoway-black-pudding"]]],["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine",[["Blodpalt","dish/blodpalt"]]],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine",[["Nam tok (food)","dish/nam-tok-food"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Suet","ingredient/suet"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Egg yolk","ingredient/egg-yolk"],["Tyrolean Speck","ingredient/tyrolean-speck"],["Blood","ingredient/blood"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Avena","ingredient/avena"],["Liver as food","ingredient/liver-as-food"],["Livestock","ingredient/livestock"],["Oatmeal","ingredient/oatmeal"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4e/Pig%27s_blood_cakes.jpg/500px-Pig%27s_blood_cakes.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Carrie Kellenberger from Banciao, Taiwan","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pig's_blood_cakes.jpg"}},"dish/boulaghmane":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/boulaghmane","n":"Boulaghmane","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#e46a3f","intro":[["Boulaghmane is a Mauritanian and Western Saharan cuisine dish that consists of toasted barley flour and sugar, which is bound together with either clarified butter, clarified camel hump fat, or a vegetable oil, along with water; the final dish is either served as a paste, or formed into balls. An unleavened and uncooked dish, it is a traditional subsistence food for desert nomads in the Western Sahara and western Mauritania, as well being a common breakfast food for suhoor on Ramadan."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-boulaghmane"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boulaghmane"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Mauritanian cuisine","cuisine/mauritanian-cuisine"],["Western Saharan cuisine","cuisine/western-saharan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Mauritanian cuisine","links":[["Couscous","dish/couscous"]]}]},"ingredient/rennet":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/rennet","n":"Rennet","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Rennet () is a complex set of enzymes produced in the stomachs of ruminant mammals. Its key component is chymosin, a protease enzyme that curdles the casein proteins in milk. Rennet also contains other enzymes such as pepsin and a lipase."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rennet"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rennet"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Pecorino","dish/pecorino"]]],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",[["Cuajada","dish/cuajada"]]],["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine",[["Ostkaka","dish/ostkaka"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Ewe's milk","ingredient/ewe-s-milk"],["Raw milk","ingredient/raw-milk"],["Almonds","ingredient/almonds"],["Bitter almond","ingredient/bitter-almond"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Saffron","ingredient/saffron"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Sheep milk","ingredient/sheep-milk"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/01/Clothbound_Cheddar_-_Rennet_%284790811140%29.jpg/500px-Clothbound_Cheddar_-_Rennet_%284790811140%29.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Rebecca Siegel","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clothbound_Cheddar_-_Rennet_(4790811140).jpg"}},"ingredient/brine":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/brine","n":"Brine","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Brine (or briny water) is a high-concentration solution of salt (typically sodium chloride or calcium chloride) in water. In diverse contexts, brine may refer to the salt solutions ranging from about 3.5% (a typical concentration of seawater, on the lower end of that of solutions used for brining foods) up to about 26% (a typical saturated solution, depending on temperature). Brine forms naturally due to evaporation of ground saline water but it is also generated in the mining of sodium chloride. Brine is used for food processing and cooking (pickling and brining), for de-icing of roads and other structures, and in a number of technological processes. It is also a by-product of many industrial processes, such as desalination, so it requires wastewater treatment for proper disposal or further utilization (fresh water recovery)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-brine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"]]],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"]]],["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine",[["Doenjang","dish/doenjang"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Rassolnik","dish/rassolnik"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Meju","ingredient/meju"],["Soybean","ingredient/soybean"],["Curing (food preservation)","ingredient/curing-food-preservation"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Hard-boiled egg","ingredient/hard-boiled-egg"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"],["Kidney","ingredient/kidney"],["Kidneys as food","ingredient/kidneys-as-food"],["Liver as food","ingredient/liver-as-food"],["Pearl barley","ingredient/pearl-barley"],["Pickled cucumber","ingredient/pickled-cucumber"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/98/Water_salinity_diagram.png/500px-Water_salinity_diagram.png","w":500,"h":701,"by":"Peter Summerlin","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Water_salinity_diagram.png"}},"dish/hamantash":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/hamantash","n":"Hamantash","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Jewish cuisine","dot":"#a99fb5","intro":[["A hamantash is an Ashkenazi Jewish triangular filled-pocket biscuit associated with the Jewish holiday of Purim. The name refers to Haman, the villain in the Purim story. In Hebrew, hamantashen are also known as אוזני המן (oznei Haman), meaning \"Haman's ears\". \"Haman's ears\" also refers to a Sephardic Purim biscuit, \"Orejas de Haman\", thought to originate in Spain and Italy, that is made by frying twisted or rolled strips of dough."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-hamantash"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamantash"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Papaver","ingredient/papaver"],["Poppy seed","ingredient/poppy-seed"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Tori Avey: Jewish Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"toriavey.com","note":"Tori Avey's Jewish recipe index, often paired with notes on each dish's history.","url":"https://toriavey.com/jewish-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"The Nosher (My Jewish Learning)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"myjewishlearning.com","note":"Recipe section of the nonprofit My Jewish Learning, covering Ashkenazi, Sephardi and Mizrahi home cooking and holiday food.","url":"https://www.myjewishlearning.com/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Jewish cuisine","links":[["Appetizing store","dish/appetizing-store"],["Bagel","dish/bagel"],["Brisket (Jewish dish)","dish/brisket-jewish-dish"],["Cebularz","dish/cebularz"],["Challah","dish/challah"],["Forshmak","dish/forshmak"],["Gefilte fish","dish/gefilte-fish"],["Gondi (food)","dish/gondi-food"],["Gribenes","dish/gribenes"],["Holishkes","dish/holishkes"],["Jewish deli","dish/jewish-deli"],["Lekach","dish/lekach"],["Mafrum","dish/mafrum"],["Matzah brei","dish/matzah-brei"],["Miodownik","dish/miodownik"],["Pizza dolce di Beridde","dish/pizza-dolce-di-beridde"],["Reuben sandwich","dish/reuben-sandwich"],["Shirin polo","dish/shirin-polo"],["Sorrel soup","dish/sorrel-soup"],["Teiglach","dish/teiglach"],["Tsvikli","dish/tsvikli"],["Tzimmes","dish/tzimmes"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/72/DateHamantash.jpg/500px-DateHamantash.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Travellers & Tinkers","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DateHamantash.jpg"}},"dish/keumamah":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/keumamah","n":"Keumamah","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Keumamah is a traditional dried fish in Acehnese cuisine, usually made skipjack or tuna. Keumamah is also known as \"wood fish\", due to its hardness. It is preserved through several processes, from cleaning the fish to boiling, drying, and storing; therefore, keumamah can be stored for years, provided it is kept dry and free from moisture."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-keumamah"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keumamah"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Acehnese cuisine","cuisine/acehnese-cuisine"],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Bird's eye chili","ingredient/bird-s-eye-chili"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Skipjack tuna","ingredient/skipjack-tuna"],["Thunnus tonggol","ingredient/thunnus-tonggol"],["Tuna","ingredient/tuna"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Acehnese cuisine","links":[["Meuseukat","dish/meuseukat"],["Mie aceh","dish/mie-aceh"],["Mie caluk","dish/mie-caluk"],["Timphan","dish/timphan"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/e4/Keumamah.jpg/500px-Keumamah.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Si Gam","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Keumamah.jpg"}},"ingredient/krupuk-kulit":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/krupuk-kulit","n":"Krupuk kulit","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Krupuk kulit is a traditional Indonesian cattle skin krupuk (cracker). It is traditionally made from the soft inner skin of cattle (cow or water buffalo) which is diced and sun-dried until it hardens and loses most of its water content. The diced and dried skins are later fried in ample hot cooking oil until they expand similarly with bubbles and yield a crispy texture. This fried cattle skin is then sealed in vacuum plastic bags to ensure and prolong its crispiness."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-krupuk-kulit"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krupuk_kulit"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Krechek","dish/krechek"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cowhide","ingredient/cowhide"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/94/Kerupuk_kulit.JPG/500px-Kerupuk_kulit.JPG","w":500,"h":443,"by":"Midori","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kerupuk_kulit.JPG"}},"dish/ma-amoul":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ma-amoul","n":"Ma'amoul","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Arab cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Ma'amoul is a filled cookie made with semolina flour and butter. Originating in the Arab world, the filling can be made with dried fruits like figs, dates, or nuts such as pistachios, walnuts, and occasionally almonds or cheese."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ma-amoul"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ma'amoul"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Arab cuisine","cuisine/arab-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Phoenix dactylifera","ingredient/phoenix-dactylifera"],["Pistachios","ingredient/pistachios"],["Walnuts","ingredient/walnuts"]]},{"h":"More from Arab cuisine","links":[["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"],["Kibbeh safarjaliyeh","dish/kibbeh-safarjaliyeh"],["Meghli","dish/meghli"],["Shakshouka","dish/shakshouka"],["Shawarma","dish/shawarma"],["Torshi","dish/torshi"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/9e/Mamoul_biscotti_libanesi.jpg/500px-Mamoul_biscotti_libanesi.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"fugzu","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamoul_biscotti_libanesi.jpg"}},"ingredient/lung":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/lung","n":"Lung","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The lungs are the primary organs of the respiratory system in many animals, including humans. In mammals and most other tetrapods, two lungs are located near the backbone on either side of the heart. Their function in the respiratory system is to extract oxygen from the atmosphere and transfer it into the bloodstream, and to release carbon dioxide from the bloodstream into the atmosphere, in a process of gas exchange. Respiration is driven by different muscular systems in different species. Mammals, reptiles and birds use their musculoskeletal systems to support and foster breathing. In early tetrapods, air was driven into the lungs by the pharyngeal muscles via buccal pumping, a mechanism still seen in amphibians. In humans, the primary muscle that drives breathing is the diaphragm. The lungs also provide airflow that makes vocalisation including speech possible."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-lung"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lung"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Sicilian cuisine","cuisine/sicilian-cuisine",[["Pani câ meusa","dish/pani-ca-meusa"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Spleen","ingredient/spleen"]]}]},"dish/pkhali":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/pkhali","n":"Pkhali","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Georgian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Pkhali is a traditional Georgian dish of chopped and minced vegetables, made of cabbage, eggplant, spinach, beans, beets and combined with ground walnuts, vinegar, onions, garlic, and herbs. Pkhali is also called mkhali. The common ingredient of all variations of pkhali is puréed walnut sauce. In Georgian restaurants, pkhali is usually served in three types: spinach, beetroot and white beans."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-pkhali"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pkhali"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":9,"links":[["Beta","ingredient/beta"],["Brassica","ingredient/brassica"],["Eggplant","ingredient/eggplant"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"]